 ### [Normal Ray Alignment Loss](4202-normal-ray-alignment-loss.md)
-Normal Ray Alignment Loss is a regularization term used in differentiable volume rendering that encourages predicted surface normals at sample points to align or anti-align with the viewing ray direction. It solves the problem of ambiguous or inconsistent normal orientations in learned radiance fields by enforcing geometric consistency between the rendered geometry and the camera viewpoint. It is distinguished from sibling methods like Curvature Loss by directly optimizing the dot product between normals and rays rather than penalizing geometric smoothness or second-order derivatives.
+Normal Ray Alignment Loss is a regularization term that encourages predicted normals at sample points to align or anti-align with the viewing ray direction. It enforces geometric consistency between rendered normals and camera viewpoints.
+
+### [2D Planar Image Alignment](6845-2d-planar-image-alignment.md)
+2D Planar Image Alignment is a simplified joint optimization problem used to demonstrate the robustness of differentiable rendering pipelines by aligning overlapping 2D image patches with learned content. It solves the problem of reconstructing a ground truth image from transformed patches by simultaneously optimizing the image content representation and the geometric transformation parameters. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Curvature Loss](2911-curvature-loss.md) by focusing on geometric consistency via homography warps rather than smoothness priors on the field itself, serving as a 2D analog to the 3D alignment losses in [Geometric and Normal Alignment Losses](4970-geometric-and-normal-alignment-losses.md).
 
 ### [Material Smoothness Loss](7299-material-smoothness-loss.md)
-Material Smoothness Loss is a regularization term applied to material fields in inverse rendering to enforce spatial smoothness by penalizing color differences between neighboring surface points. It addresses the issue of noisy or high-frequency artifacts in reconstructed material properties (such as albedo or BRDF parameters) by encouraging continuity across the surface geometry. It differs from sibling methods like Curvature Loss, which penalizes geometric curvature, and Normal Ray Alignment Loss, which aligns predicted normals with ray directions, by focusing specifically on the smoothness of the material appearance itself rather than the underlying geometry or lighting consistency.
+Material Smoothness Loss is a regularization term applied to material fields to enforce spatial smoothness by penalizing color differences between neighboring surface points. It addresses the ambiguity between material properties and illumination in inverse rendering by constraining material gradients to correspond with image edges, thereby preventing noisy and inconsistent material reconstructions. It differs from standard smoothness losses by utilizing image gradients as bilateral weights to preserve material boundaries where the image shows sharp transitions.
 
