 ## Background
-Geometric and normal alignment losses emerged as essential components in the training of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and related differentiable rendering techniques to mitigate the "floaters" and geometric ambiguities inherent in volume rendering. Early works like NeRF [S5] relied heavily on photometric loss, which often resulted in plausible colors but poor geometry. Subsequent research introduced curvature regularization [S1, unofficial] and normal consistency losses [S2, unofficial] to enforce structural priors. The distinction between optimizing for color fidelity and geometric correctness became a central theme, leading to specialized losses like Boundary Loss for architectural scenes [S4] and Alignment-Aware losses for handling pose uncertainty [S3]. These methods are now standard in inverse rendering pipelines to ensure that the learned radiance fields represent coherent 3D structures.
+Geometric and normal alignment losses have emerged as critical components in differentiable rendering pipelines to resolve the inherent ambiguity between geometry and appearance. Early works in neural radiance fields (NeRF) relied heavily on photometric loss, which often resulted in "floaters" and incorrect geometry in textureless regions [S3]. To address this, researchers introduced curvature-based regularization to smooth scalar fields [S1, unofficial] and normal alignment constraints to ensure consistency between predicted normals and ray directions [S1, unofficial]. The introduction of predicted normal heads coupled with density gradients further refined geometric fidelity [S2]. In indoor scenarios, boundary losses were proposed to leverage architectural depth priors, enforcing planar constraints that standard losses ignore [S3]. Additionally, alignment-aware patch matching losses were developed to handle spatial misalignments in high-fidelity training [S2]. The sensitivity of these losses to false geometry has been analyzed, showing that distortion and CF losses can be particularly sensitive to incorrect geometric structures [S4]. In scientific volume rendering, similar principles are applied to vector fields using Jacobian and cosine direction losses to maintain physical fidelity [S5, unofficial].
 
